# The build context is uploaded to ACR, so anything not excluded here leaves the machine.
# Keep this at least as strict as .gitignore.
#
# No trailing slashes, and that is not a style choice. Docker reads `web` and `web/` the
# same way; `az acr build` does not. Its IgnoreRule strips a trailing slash only for `!`
# exceptions, so `web/` compiles to `^web/$` while a directory entry in the tar is named
# `web`, and the rule can never match. Measured on 19 August 2026: the context was
# 116.8 MiB and carried .azure/ (including the Azure token cache) and web/node_modules;
# `secrets.local.yaml`, the single line written without a slash, was the only rule that
# took effect.

.git
.github
.azure
.venv
build
dist
*.egg-info
__pycache__
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache

# Never leaves this machine.
pictures
secrets.local.yaml
quota-requests.local.yaml
# What a house with no person in it wrote: the screens, the sheets, and the transcript of
# an afternoon. The transcript is the reason this line exists.
pretend
hack_plan
microsoftinfo
_reference
notes

# Not needed to run the panel.
docs
ideas
infra
scripts
tests
tools
firmware
# The browser tier is built by its own workflow and served by Static Web Apps. The
# Dockerfile copies none of it, and its node_modules alone is 166 MB of context to upload.
web
vision
printing
*.md
!README.md
